<Base title="Editorial Policy" description="How the Heraldry Project uses original sources, translations, documented objects and visible uncertainty.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Sources before stories" title="How the project is edited." description="Heraldry belongs to many places, periods and languages. The evidence should remain visible wherever the teaching takes us." />
  <section class="section"><article class="wrap world-article">
    <h2>A growing research corpus</h2>
    <p>The project begins with things a reader can inspect: a shield, a manuscript leaf, a catalogue entry, or an institutional rule. We explain the terms needed to work with that evidence, then ask what the evidence supports. New material belongs here when it helps answer another useful question and its sources can be checked.</p>
    <h2>Read the originals</h2>
    <p>Archival catalogues, statutory texts, registers, seals, armorial manuscripts and historical scholarship should be traceable in their original language. Records retain their title, institution, locator, source link and consultation date. An English explanation lets a reader begin working with a source, but the original wording remains necessary when the argument depends on what the document actually says.</p>
    <h2>Keep different kinds of writing distinct</h2>
    <p>Original editorial synthesis, translated summaries, short attributed quotations and licensed or public-domain translations serve different purposes. The project labels them accordingly. Working translations and unresolved readings remain provisional until the stated review has actually occurred.</p>
    <h2>Preserve context and disagreement</h2>
    <p>Rules vary across jurisdictions and centuries. Evidence of historical use, a catalogue identification, a legal entitlement and a modern drawing are different claims. A useful explanation identifies the source of a claim and carries its qualification forward. If a catalogue offers two possible families, the teaching should explain what might distinguish them rather than quietly selecting one.</p>
    <h2>Document the objects and respect their rights</h2>
    <p>Teaching diagrams are distinguished from authentic dated objects. A photograph has a maker, a date, and reuse terms of its own, separate from the age of the arms it depicts. Keeping those details beside the image lets a reader distinguish the object from the modern reproduction and trace each part of the argument.</p>
    <h2>Teach honestly and accessibly</h2>
    <p>The Academy's introductory foundation and research seminars confer no university credit or professional qualification. Software checks support publication but do not establish scholarly or specialist approval. Reading and practice should work on phones, with a keyboard and with assistive technologies, and corrections should make the evidence clearer.</p>
